Camille,<br />
<br />
I have read the May 31, 2011 letter and the December 2012 exchange. The ans=
wer is not the one the referral assumed, and it does not improve on a secon=
d reading.<br />
<br />
The operative sentence is: "This undertaking is given for calendar years 20=
11 and 2012 and shall expire by its terms on December 31, 2012." That is no=
t boilerplate at the end of a form letter. The record shows it was negotiat=
ed. Counsel asked for a defined period in May 2011, the examiner recommende=
d internally that the staff accept a commitment expressed for 2011 and 2012=
, and the closing recommendation of June 9, 2011 states that the follow-up =
flag should expire with the letter because the letter expires.<br />
<br />
It gets worse for the theory. In December 2012 the firm wrote to the branch=
 chief to say the undertaking was about to lapse and asked whether the staf=
f read it otherwise. The branch chief answered that the staff did not exten=
d undertakings, was not asking the firm to renew this one, and that the fir=
m's obligations came from the rule rather than from the letter. So the firm=
 asked, in writing, and the Commission's staff told it, in writing, that af=
ter December 31, 2012 there was no undertaking.<br />
<br />
The conduct we are looking at starts in 2023. There is no undertaking in fo=
rce in 2023 to breach. Any charge framed as a breach of the 2011 commitment=
 fails on the document, and the December 2012 exchange means we would be li=
tigating against our own branch chief's written answer.<br />
<br />
That leaves the rule. On the rule the case is ordinary and, on the current =
record, thin: we have one client complaint, we have not scoped the account =
population, and the firm has been filing Form ADV amendments the examinatio=
n staff have not yet reviewed. If we want a case here it is a 206(4)-2 case=
 that has to be built from 2023 forward on its own facts.<br />
